WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
(acoustic music)

00:00:04.258 --> 00:00:06.506
- [Voiceover] Before
I've tried basketball.

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:07.923
I tried football.

00:00:09.829 --> 00:00:13.425
Really, I just felt better
with an ax in my hand.

00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:14.735
At Southern Illinois I had the opportunity

00:00:14.735 --> 00:00:16.920
to become a lumberjack.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.322
- [Voiceover] Contestants ready!

00:00:18.322 --> 00:00:19.655
Three, two, one!

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.054
(whistle blows)

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:24.073
(saw saws)

00:00:24.073 --> 00:00:26.790
(ax chops)

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.816
- My name's Jack Cearley.

00:00:27.816 --> 00:00:28.708
I'm the president of the

00:00:28.708 --> 00:00:30.477
Southern Illinois
University Forestry Club.

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:31.645
- [Voiceover] Here you
go ladies, you got this!

00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:33.703
- [Voiceover] Come on girls, let's go!

00:00:33.703 --> 00:00:36.840
- We're at the 64th
Midwest Forestry Conclave

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.182
in Oswald, Missouri put
on by Mizzou University.

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:41.769
This year I believe
there's 11 or 12 schools

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:44.208
competing throughout the
midwest here at the Conclave.

00:00:44.208 --> 00:00:45.045
(whistle blows)

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.269
(saw saws)

00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:47.436
Some of the events going on here

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:50.436
are the underhand chop, single buck,

00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:54.651
single sawing, double sawing,

00:00:54.651 --> 00:00:58.675
jack jack saw, jack jill saw, bolt toss.

00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:01.670
We also do events like tree ID and wood ID

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.922
and traverse, where we
have to use coordinates

00:01:03.922 --> 00:01:06.809
and forestry measurements to
find our way through the woods.

00:01:06.809 --> 00:01:10.827
You know, I'm lucky if I'm
still standing when I'm done.

00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:13.588
(crowd shouts in encouragement)

00:01:13.588 --> 00:01:15.888
All the events that we
do, they all come from

00:01:15.888 --> 00:01:17.472
traditional forestry skills.

00:01:17.472 --> 00:01:20.515
So this is our way of keeping
all those skills alive.

00:01:20.515 --> 00:01:22.200
Forestry, I always like to think about

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.451
the old Greek proverb,
"Society grows great

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:27.444
"when men plant trees whose
shade they'll never sit in."

00:01:27.444 --> 00:01:29.696
And that really speaks to
me when it comes to forestry

00:01:29.696 --> 00:01:32.216
because in forestry, we're
working very long term scales.

00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:33.716
I'm gonna maybe put forth management plans

00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:35.867
that can effect my kids or my grandkids,

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:38.066
and so if I put in a
trail I wanna make sure

00:01:38.066 --> 00:01:39.458
that trail is done right,
if we're gonna cut timber

00:01:39.458 --> 00:01:41.753
I wanna make trees get planted afterwards.

00:01:41.753 --> 00:01:45.451
You know it's a very long term process.

00:01:45.451 --> 00:01:46.996
When you're out there,
announcer says your name

00:01:46.996 --> 00:01:50.692
over the speakers, and the
whole team just cheers,

00:01:50.692 --> 00:01:52.941
your family's screamin' and
you can't do anything but smile

00:01:52.941 --> 00:01:54.251
and you just want to grab the ax and you

00:01:54.251 --> 00:01:56.889
wanna chop the wood as fast as you can.

00:01:56.889 --> 00:01:59.735
This is just where I belong.

00:01:59.735 --> 00:02:01.985
(ax chops)

00:02:02.903 --> 00:02:05.403
(birds chirp)

